WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.749
Welcome to Cyrus.

2
00:00:01.749 --> 00:00:05.151
In this first video, we'll find our way around, connect

3
00:00:05.151 --> 00:00:08.305
an exchange, and go through your profile settings.

4
00:00:08.305 --> 00:00:11.616
Everything here is demo data, not a real account.

5
00:00:12.284 --> 00:00:16.867
Down the left side is the page rail: your portfolio pages at the top,

6
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:21.316
then Automations and the Balancer, and your Profile at the bottom.

7
00:00:21.999 --> 00:00:25.606
Across the top, the search box jumps to any page.

8
00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:30.486
The exchange selector chooses between all your accounts combined, or just one.

9
00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:34.667
The worker pill shows the automation engine is running,

10
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.309
and Ask opens the AI assistant, which has its own video.

11
00:00:38.975 --> 00:00:42.436
Setting up starts on your profile, at the bottom of the rail.

12
00:00:43.112 --> 00:00:46.746
At the top, a chip shows how many exchanges are connected,

13
00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.565
and warns you if any haven't been validated.

14
00:00:50.221 --> 00:00:52.724
Settings are split into four tabs.

15
00:00:52.724 --> 00:00:56.248
Let's start with the one that matters most, Exchanges.

16
00:00:56.934 --> 00:01:00.525
Each card is one connection: the exchange, your label

17
00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:03.506
for it, and whether its keys are validated.

18
00:01:04.162 --> 00:01:07.968
Test checks the keys by asking the exchange for your balance.

19
00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:11.319
If that works, the connection is marked validated.

20
00:01:11.319 --> 00:01:14.750
If the keys are rejected, it's marked not validated.

21
00:01:15.423 --> 00:01:18.511
The bin removes a connection and its stored keys.

22
00:01:19.171 --> 00:01:23.988
To connect an account, scroll down to Add a connection and choose the exchange.

23
00:01:23.988 --> 00:01:29.387
A question mark appears, linking to that exchange's guide to creating API keys.

24
00:01:30.058 --> 00:01:35.553
The label tells your accounts apart, because you can connect the same exchange more than once.

25
00:01:35.553 --> 00:01:37.335
Let's call this one Long term.

26
00:01:38.005 --> 00:01:40.601
Just make sure each one is a different account.

27
00:01:40.601 --> 00:01:44.227
Connections are added together, so the same account twice

28
00:01:44.227 --> 00:01:47.343
would count double, and reusing keys is blocked.

29
00:01:48.013 --> 00:01:53.362
Next, create an API key on the exchange, with only what Cyrus needs.

30
00:01:53.362 --> 00:01:57.680
On Kraken: query funds, query open and closed orders

31
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.833
and trades, and create, modify and cancel orders.

32
00:02:02.498 --> 00:02:06.012
Only add withdraw funds if you plan to use withdraw rules.

33
00:02:06.012 --> 00:02:09.262
Kraken is the only exchange Cyrus can withdraw from,

34
00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:12.262
and only to addresses you've whitelisted there.

35
00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:16.627
For Coinbase, create the key in Coinbase Advanced, not

36
00:02:16.627 --> 00:02:20.110
the standard app, with view and trade permissions.

37
00:02:20.778 --> 00:02:25.728
On Binance, enable reading and spot trading, and leave withdrawals off.

38
00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:30.266
On Gemini, use the Trader role, Gemini's default.

39
00:02:30.266 --> 00:02:37.008
Gemini also has a sandbox, a practice exchange with fake funds, so a sandbox option appears.

40
00:02:37.691 --> 00:02:41.218
Tick it only if your key came from Gemini's sandbox site.

41
00:02:41.218 --> 00:02:45.434
Cyrus then connects to the test exchange instead of the real one.

42
00:02:46.126 --> 00:02:48.350
Robinhood doesn't give you a secret.

43
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.751
Instead, you create a pair of keys, give Robinhood

44
00:02:51.751 --> 00:02:54.879
the public half, and it gives you an API key.

45
00:02:55.549 --> 00:02:58.597
Cyrus offers a small key generator for that.

46
00:02:58.597 --> 00:03:03.132
It needs Python 3, works offline, and prints both keys.

47
00:03:03.132 --> 00:03:05.738
The private one goes in the field below.

48
00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:10.761
Whichever exchange it is, paste the API key and secret here.

49
00:03:10.761 --> 00:03:12.636
Both stay hidden as you type.

50
00:03:13.304 --> 00:03:18.227
When you click Add Connection, the keys are encrypted and saved on your own computer.

51
00:03:18.227 --> 00:03:23.759
They're only decrypted when Cyrus talks to that exchange, and never sent anywhere else.

52
00:03:24.439 --> 00:03:27.441
Cyrus tests the new connection straight away, and

53
00:03:27.441 --> 00:03:30.320
once it's validated it appears across the app.

54
00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.325
This is a demo, so we'll clear the form instead.

55
00:03:33.997 --> 00:03:37.285
Further down, Stock market data is optional.

56
00:03:37.285 --> 00:03:43.960
Add a free Alpaca key here, and the Charts page can show US stocks and ETFs as well as crypto.

57
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.729
The Charts video covers it.

58
00:03:46.394 --> 00:03:49.456
The Assistant tab switches on the AI assistant.

59
00:03:49.456 --> 00:03:53.416
It's off until you add your own Anthropic API key, which Cyrus

60
00:03:53.416 --> 00:03:57.248
checks with Anthropic and encrypts like your exchange keys.

61
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.295
It's the one feature that sends portfolio data off your

62
00:04:01.295 --> 00:04:04.240
computer, straight to Anthropic under your key.

63
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.966
The assistant video covers it in detail.

64
00:04:07.641 --> 00:04:13.189
On Notifications, desktop notifications tell you when an automation rule runs.

65
00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:16.434
The second switch hides the support button in the corner.

66
00:04:17.099 --> 00:04:22.342
Cyrus can also email you when a rule executes, sent from your own email account.

67
00:04:22.342 --> 00:04:26.038
Turn on Email Me on Execution and enter your address.

68
00:04:26.712 --> 00:04:29.885
For the password, use an app password from your email

69
00:04:29.885 --> 00:04:32.939
provider's security settings, not your normal one.

70
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.760
The link underneath shows how.

71
00:04:35.439 --> 00:04:39.108
For Gmail, Outlook, Yahoo, iCloud and other common

72
00:04:39.108 --> 00:04:42.703
providers, the mail server is filled in for you.

73
00:04:42.703 --> 00:04:44.498
Otherwise, enter it here.

74
00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:47.832
Send Test checks the form before you save.

75
00:04:47.832 --> 00:04:50.584
Emails only go out while Cyrus is running.

76
00:04:51.278 --> 00:04:55.987
Once email is on and saved, you also get a monthly report: your holdings,

77
00:04:55.987 --> 00:05:00.696
that month's automation log, and where your rules and orders move money.

78
00:05:00.696 --> 00:05:04.818
If Cyrus was closed at month end, it goes out next time it runs.

79
00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:10.991
Send Test Report emails you one right away, without counting as that month's report.

80
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.759
Finally, the Account tab, for your username and password.

81
00:05:17.105 --> 00:05:20.619
Dark Mode switches between the dark and light themes.

82
00:05:22.222 --> 00:05:24.699
Account Active is a master switch.

83
00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:29.871
Turn it off, and automations and exchange features stop until you turn it back on.

84
00:05:30.536 --> 00:05:35.300
That's it: connect an exchange, test it, and set up the extras you want.

85
00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.302
Everything is stored in one database file on your own computer.

86
00:05:39.302 --> 00:05:41.470
Next, a tour of the Overview.

